== Background ==
{{See also|2017 Serbian presidential election|2020 Serbian parliamentary election|2018–2020 Serbian protests|2020 Serbian protests}}
A [[Populism|populist]] coalition led by the [[Serbian Progressive Party]] (SNS) and supported by the [[Socialist Party of Serbia]] (SPS), came to power after the [[2012 Serbian parliamentary election|2012 parliamentary election]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=10 May 2012|title=Izbori 2012: Rezultati i postizborna trgovina|url=https://www.vreme.com/projekat/izbori-2012-rezultati-i-postizborna-trgovina/|access-date=10 February 2022|website=Vreme|language=sr-RS}}</ref> [[Aleksandar Vučić]], who was appointed as [[Deputy Prime Minister of Serbia|deputy prime minister]] in 2012, was elected [[Prime Minister of Serbia|prime minister]] after the [[2014 Serbian parliamentary election|2014 election]], and finally elected as president in [[2017 Serbian presidential election|2017]], winning 55% of the popular vote.<ref>{{cite web |last=Rudić |first=Filip |date=2 April 2017 |title=Vučić wins Serbian Presidential elections |url=https://balkaninsight.com/2017/04/02/vucic-wins-serbian-presidential-elections-04-02-2017-1/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=Balkan Insight}}</ref> Since he came to power, observers have assessed that Serbia has suffered from [[democratic backsliding]] into [[authoritarianism]], followed by a decline in [[Media freedom in Serbia|media freedom]] and [[civil liberties]].<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Bieber|first1=Florian|author-link=Florian Bieber|date=July 2018|title=Patterns of competitive authoritarianism in the Western Balkans|journal=East European Politics|volume=38|issue=3|pages=337–54|doi=10.1080/21599165.2018.1490272|doi-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|last1=Maerz|first1=Seraphine F|display-authors=etal|date=April 2020|title=State of the world 2019: autocratization surges – resistance grows|journal=Democratization|volume=27|issue=6|pages=909–927|doi=10.1080/13510347.2020.1758670|doi-access=free}}</ref> [[2017 Serbian protests|Mass protests]] began after his election in 2017 due to the alleged dominant control of Serbia's media by Vučić.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Da Silva |first1=Chantal |author-link=Chantal Da Silva |date=8 April 2017 |title=Serbian protesters accuse media of turning blind eye, as anti-corruption rallies continue |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/europe/serbia-protests-media-aleksandar-vucic-prime-minister-police-a7673532.html |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220506/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/europe/serbia-protests-media-aleksandar-vucic-prime-minister-police-a7673532.html |archive-date=6 May 2022 |access-date=9 April 2017 |website=The Independent}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=9 April 2017 |title=A Serbian Election Erodes Democracy |website=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/04/09/opinion/a-serbian-election-erodes-democracy.html?_r=0 |access-date=9 April 2017}}</ref> Subsequently, he appointed [[Ana Brnabić]] as the new head of government,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Surk |first=Barbara |date=28 June 2017 |title=Serbia Gets Its First Female, and First Openly Gay, Premier |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/06/28/world/europe/serbia-ana-brnabic-prime-minister.html |access-date=23 June 2020 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> who initially served as an [[Independent politician|independent]] but later joined SNS in 2019.<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 October 2019 |title=PM Brnabic joins Vucic's ruling Serbian Progressive Party |url=http://rs.n1info.com/English/NEWS/a533518/PM-Brnabic-joins-Vucic-s-ruling-Serbian-Progressive-Party.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191031110958/http://rs.n1info.com/English/NEWS/a533518/PM-Brnabic-joins-Vucic-s-ruling-Serbian-Progressive-Party.html |archive-date=31 October 2019 |access-date=23 June 2020 |website=N1}}</ref>

The assassination of opposition politician [[Oliver Ivanović]] and an assault on another opposition politician [[Borko Stefanović]] triggered the start in late November 2018 of [[2018–2020 Serbian protests|long-lasting anti-government protests]],<ref>{{cite news |date=16 January 2018 |title=Prominent Kosovo Serb politician Oliver Ivanovic killed in drive-by shooting |newspaper=The Telegraph |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2018/01/16/prominent-kosovo-serb-politician-oliver-ivanovic-killed-drive/ |access-date=30 January 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=23 November 2018 |title=Borku Stefanoviću razbijena glava tokom tribine u Kruševcu |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/a438333-borko-stefanovic-napadnut-u-krusevcu/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite news|date=8 December 2018|title=Thousands protest in Serbia over attack on opposition politician|language=en|work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-serbia-protests-idUSKBN1O70S7|access-date=23 January 2019}}</ref><ref name=":4">{{Cite news |last1=Santora |first1=Marc |last2=Engelbrecht |first2=Cora |last3=De Guzman |first3=Orlando |last4=Laffin |first4=Ben |date=19 January 2019 |title=Serbian Protesters Fear Creeping Authoritarianism |language=en-US |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/video/world/europe/100000006309082/serbia-democracy-protest-putin.html |access-date=23 January 2019 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> which ended in March 2020 due to the proclamation of [[COVID-19 lockdowns]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=10 March 2020|title=Protesti subotom se obustavljaju dok se ne popravi epidemiološka situacija|url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/a576762-Protesti-subotom-se-obustavljaju-dok-se-ne-popravi-epidemioloska-situacija/|access-date=30 January 2022|website=N1|language=sr-RS}}</ref> These protests helped to strengthen the unity of opposition forces, of which the [[Alliance for Serbia]] (SZS) was the biggest and most prominent,<ref>{{cite web |date=10 August 2020 |title=Formirana Udružena opozicija Srbije, cilj smena bahatog režima |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/formirana-udruzena-opozicija-srbije/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr}}</ref><ref name=":10">{{Cite web |date=29 December 2018 |title=Thousands march against Serbia's president for 4th week |url=https://apnews.com/f289bfd8bd894096848e64995440418d |access-date=23 January 2019 |website=Associated Press}}</ref> while demonstrators demanded the resignation of Vučić and other senior officials.<ref>{{Cite news|date=12 January 2019|title=Protests against Serbia's President Vucic enter sixth week|language=en|work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-serbia-protests-idUSKCN1P60PF|access-date=23 January 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=23 December 2018|title=Protesters Call On Serbian President To Resign|url=https://www.rferl.org/a/serbia-belgrade-opposition-protest-march-third-consecutive/29672069.html|access-date=5 February 2019|website=Radio Free Europe|language=en-US}}</ref> Parallel to this, Vučić launched the "Future of Serbia" rallies in 2019.<ref name="FutureForSerbia">{{cite web |date=9 February 2019 |title=Protests against President Vucic spreads throughout Serbia |url=https://www.france24.com/en/20190209-protests-against-president-vucic-spreads-throughout-serbia |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=France24}}</ref> Journalist Slobodan Georgiev noted that the rallies effectively silenced the protests.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Georgiev |first=Slobodan |date=24 April 2019 |title=Vucic Rally May Have Silenced Serbia's Protest Movement |url=https://balkaninsight.com/2019/04/24/vucic-rally-may-have-silenced-serbias-protest-movement/ |access-date=17 January 2023 |website=Balkan Insight |language=en-US}}</ref> SZS declared that they would [[Election boycott|boycott]] the [[2020 Serbian parliamentary election|2020 parliamentary election]], citing that the conditions were not free and fair.<ref>{{Cite web|date=16 September 2019|title=Opposition Alliance for Serbia declares boycott|url=https://rs.n1info.com/english/news/a526261-Opposition-Alliance-for-Serbia-declares-boycott/|access-date=30 January 2022|website=N1}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=3 September 2019 |title=Organizatori protesta "1 od 5 miliona" pozvali na bojkot izbora |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/organizatori-protesta-1-od-5-miliona-pozvali-na-bojkot-izbora-video/ |access-date=3 September 2019 |website=Danas |language=sr}}</ref>

The SNS-led ballot list, named "[[SNS coalition|For Our Children]]", won a supermajority of votes and seats in the June 2020 parliamentary election,<ref>{{Cite web|date=22 June 2020|title=Rezultati izbora 2020 i reakcije|url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/a612402-Rezultati-izbora-2020-i-reakcije/|access-date=30 January 2022|website=N1|language=sr-RS}}</ref> while the government was formed in late October 2020.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Zorić |first1=Ognjen |last2=Martinović |first2=Iva |date=20 October 2020 |title=Vučić: Opet izbori 2022, Vlada ograničenog trajanja, Dačić na čelu Skupštine |url=https://www.slobodnaevropa.org/a/brnabic-predlozila-novi-sastav-vlade-srbije/30903131.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201022190654/https://www.slobodnaevropa.org/a/brnabic-predlozila-novi-sastav-vlade-srbije/30903131.html |archive-date=22 October 2020 |newspaper=Radio Slobodna Evropa |language=sr}}</ref> Following the election, Vučić stated that snap parliamentary elections would be held in or before April 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 October 2020 |title=Vučić: Vanredni parlamentarni izbori najkasnije 3. aprila 2022. |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/a662868-Vucic-Vanredni-parlamentarni-izbori-najkasnije-3-aprila-2022/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> Shortly after the election ended, [[Serbian protests (2020–)|protests]] erupted in [[Belgrade]].<ref name="bbc2">{{Cite web |date=7 July 2020 |title=Protesti u Srbiji: Desetine povređenih i privedenih, suzavac, kamenice i pendreci i u Novom Sadu, Nišu i Kragujevcu |url=https://www.bbc.com/serbian/lat/srbija-53329591 |access-date=9 July 2020 |website=BBC News |language=sr}}</ref> These anti-government protests were marked with [[police brutality]] and [[violence]] orchestrated by the government,<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 July 2020 |title=Policija na konjima ispred Skupštine, demonstranti nisu dali vatrogascima da prođu |url=https://www.novosti.rs/vesti/naslovna/politika/aktuelno.289.html:874980-Policija-na-konjima-ispred-Skupstine-demonstranti-nisu-dali-vatrogascima-da-prodju |access-date=9 July 2020 |website=Novosti |language=sr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=8 July 2020 |title=Policija pendrecima tukla muškarce koji su mirno sedeli na klupi u beogradskom parku |url=https://www.021.rs/story/Info/Srbija/247828/VIDEO-Policija-pendrecima-tukla-muskarce-koji-su-mirno-sedeli-na-klupi-u-beogradskom-parku.html |access-date=9 July 2020 |website=021.rs |language=sr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=8 July 2020 |title=Sergej Trifunović napadnut na protestu ispred Skupštine: "Pripadnici službe su želeli da bace ljagu na protest" |url=https://www.nedeljnik.rs/sergej-trifunovic-napadnut-na-protestu-ispred-skupstine-pripadnici-sluzbe-su-zeleli-da-bace-ljagu-na-protest/ |access-date=9 July 2020 |website=Nedeljnik |language=sr}}</ref> while the protesters were divided between taking peaceful and violent approaches.<ref name="bbc2" /><ref name="bbc">{{Cite news |date=8 July 2020 |title=Protesters storm Serb parliament over COVID curfew |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-53332225 |access-date=9 July 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=8 July 2020 |title=U Novom Sadu okupljeni na protestu demolirali prostorije SNS-a |language=sr |work=Glas Javnosti |url=https://www.glas-javnosti.rs/drustvo/u-novom-sadu-okupljeni-na-protestu-demolirali-prostorije-sns-a-6418 |access-date=30 January 2022}}</ref> The protests lasted until early August 2020, while some sporadic protests continued to be organised until late 2020.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 August 2020 |title=Eggs and tomatoes thrown to the Assembly |url=https://www.b92.net/eng/news/politics.php?yyyy=2020&mm=08&dd=03&nav_id=109051 |access-date=14 August 2020 |website=B92 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=2 December 2020|title=Protest malih privrednika, blokiran centar Beograda|url=https://rs.n1info.com/biznis/a678942-Protest-malih-privrednika-blokiran-centar-Beograda/|access-date=30 January 2022|website=N1|language=sr-RS}}</ref> SZS was challenged with inter-coalition conflicts that remained active until its dissolution in August 2020.<ref name=":1">{{cite web |last=Petrović |first=Ivan |date=12 August 2020 |title=Udružena opozicija Srbije – početak ne obećava |url=https://dw.com/sr/udru%C5%BEena-opozicija-srbije-po%C4%8Detak-ne-obe%C4%87ava/a-54536747 |access-date=29 August 2022 |website=Deutsche Welle |location=Belgrade |language=sr}}</ref> The [[United Opposition of Serbia]] (UOPS) was formed as a direct successor to the SZS shortly after.<ref name=":1" /> The coalition was also faced with inter-coalition conflicts, and due to their ideological differences, the coalition ended up being dissolved between December 2020 and January 2021.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{cite web |last=Valtner |first=Lidija |date=19 December 2020 |title=Zdravko Ponoš: Udružena opozicija je trenutno u blokadi |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/zdravko-ponos-udruzena-opozicija-je-trenutno-u-blokadi |access-date=29 August 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=21 January 2021|title=Aleksić: Udružena opozicija Srbije više ne funkcioniše|url=http://rtv.rs/sr_lat/politika/aleksic-udruzena-opozicija-srbije-vise-ne-funkcionise_1200389.html|access-date=30 January 2022|website=Radio Television of Vojvodina|language=sr}}</ref> It was split into two blocs, which were headed by the [[Party of Freedom and Justice]] (SSP) and [[People's Party (Serbia, 2017)|People's Party]] (Narodna).<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 January 2021 |title=Narodna stranka predstavila svoje zahteve o izbornim uslovima i "oprostila se" od Udružene opozicije Srbije (UOS) |url=https://www.blic.rs/vesti/politika/objavljena-jos-jedna-platforma-narodna-stranka-predstavila-svoje-zahteve-o-izbornim/3gc3nh4 |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=Blic |language=sr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Vlajović |first=Ivana |date=13 February 2021 |title=Opozicija oko SSP i DS predstavila platformu za dijalog o izbornim uslovima |url=https://insajder.net/sr/sajt/vazno/22750/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=Insajder |language=sr |archive-date=27 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211127110652/https://insajder.net/sr/sajt/vazno/22750/ |url-status=dead}}</ref>

=== Inter-party dialogues on electoral conditions ===
The dialogues between opposition and government parties to improve election conditions began on 28 April 2021.<ref>{{cite web |last=Popović |first=A. |date=28 April 2021 |title=Vučić na prvom međustranačkom dijalogu o izbornim uslovima |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/potvrdjeno-prisustvo-vucica-na-prvom-medjustranackom-dijalogu-o-izbornim-uslovima/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=3 May 2021 |title=Dačić: Međustranački dijalog nakon praznika, Vučić će se odazvati pozivima za učešće |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/dacic-medjustranacki-dijalog-nakon-praznika-vucic-ce-se-odazvati-pozivima-za-ucesce/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr}}</ref> Opposition parties that had negative opinions regarding the European Union decided to not participate in talks with delegators from the [[European Parliament]].<ref>{{cite web |date=4 February 2021 |title=Dijalog o izborima na dva koloseka: Deo opozicije protiv mešanja stranaca i vodiće razgovore sa vlašću bez posredovanja Evropske unije |url=https://www.novosti.rs/vesti/politika/961447/dijalog-izborima-dva-koloseka-deo-opozicije-protiv-mesanja-stranaca-vodice-razgovore-vlascu-bez-posredovanja-evropske-unije |access-date=4 February 2021 |website=Novosti |language=sr}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Valtner |first=Lidija |date=20 April 2021 |title=Posle SNS i SPS, na razgovore idu SVM i PUPS |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/posle-sns-i-sps-na-razgovore-idu-svm-i-pups/ |access-date=29 August 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr}}</ref> Parties that declined to take part in talks with delegators from the European Parliament were a part of work board dialogues, which began on 18 May 2021.<ref>{{cite web |date=18 May 2021 |title=Završen prvi sastanak Radne grupe za međustranački dijalog bez posrednika iz EU |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/zavrsen-prvi-sastanak-radne-grupe-za-medjustranacki-dijalog-bez-posrednika-iz-eu/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr}}</ref> The talks with delegators from the European Parliament began on 9 July 2021.<ref>{{cite web |last=Novaković |first=Ana |date=8 July 2021 |title=Nastavak međustranačkog dijaloga – vlast spremna, opozicija ne očekuje previše |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/nastavak-medjustranackog-dijaloga-vlast-spremna-opozicija-ne-ocekuje-previse/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr}}</ref>

[[Member of the European Parliament|Members of the European Parliament]] [[Tanja Fajon]], [[Vladimír Bilčík]] and [[Knut Fleckenstein]] met with Vučić in Belgrade on 9 July 2021.<ref>{{cite web |last=Valtner |first=Lidija |date=9 July 2021 |title=Evroparlamentarci prvo s Vučićem pa sa opozicijom |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/evroparlamentarci-prvo-s-vucicem-pa-sa-opozicijom/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr}}</ref> Soon after, the discussion began with [[Vladimir Orlić]], [[Sandra Božić]], and [[Marijan Rističević]] representing SNS.<ref>{{cite web |date=9 July 2021 |title=Vučić na dijalog poslao Orlića, Sandru Božić i Rističevića |url=https://nova.rs/vesti/politika/vucic-se-sastao-sa-fajon-bilcikom-i-flekenstajnom/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=Nova S |language=sr}}</ref> After the meeting, delegators filed a proposal to improve electoral conditions.<ref>{{cite web |last=Gajić |first=Petar |date=10 July 2021 |title=Delegacija EP daće predlog za poboljšanje izbornih uslova, optimizam nakon runde |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/delegacija-ep-dace-predlog-za-poboljsanje-izbornih-uslova-optimizam-nakon-runde/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr}}</ref> The draft document was released to the parties on 1 September 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 September 2021 |title=Posrednici iz EU usvojili nacrt radnog dokumenta za međustranački dijalog |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/posrednici-iz-eu-usvojili-nacrt-radnog-dokumenta-za-medjustranacki-dijalog/ |access-date=13 September 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> SSP, Narodna, [[Movement for Reversal]] (PZP), [[Movement of Free Citizens (Serbia)|Movement of Free Citizens]] (PSG), [[New Party (Serbia)|New Party]] (Nova), and the [[Together for Serbia]] (ZZS) opposition political parties criticised the draft document, while Vučić stated that "he isn't thrilled" with the document.<ref name=":9">{{Cite web |last=Sovrlić |first=Sanja |date=9 September 2021 |title=Teško prihvatljiv za vlast i opoziciju - predlog evroparlamentaraca "podbacio" |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/tesko-prihvatljiv-za-vlast-i-opoziciju-predlog-evroparlamentaraca-podbacio/ |access-date=13 September 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Radišić |first=Nikola |date=8 September 2021 |title=Zelenović: Čujem da su svi nezadovoljni, ali ne može Fajon da pobedi umesto nas |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/zelenovic-i-veselinovic-o-predlogu-evroparlamentaraca/ |access-date=13 September 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=10 September 2021 |title=Narodna stranka odbacila dokument evropskih posrednika o izbornim uslovima |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/narodna-stranka-odbacila-dokument-evropskih-posrednika-o-izbornim-uslovima/ |access-date=13 September 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> Vučić and Dačić met again with the delegators on 17 September;<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nešić |first=Nenad |date=17 September 2021 |title=Nova runda međustranačkog dijaloga uz posredovanje evroparlamentaraca |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/u-toku-nova-runda-medjustranackog-dijaloga-uz-posredovanje-evroparlamentaraca/ |access-date=21 September 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> Vučić and [[Đorđe Milićević]], the head of the SPS parliamentary group, confirmed that they would accept the suggestions in the new draft document.<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 September 2021 |title=Milićević: SPS spreman za konsenzus, dokument evroparlamentaraca je dobar |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/milicevic-sps-spreman-za-konsenzus-dokument-evroparlamentaraca-je-dobar/ |access-date=21 September 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=17 September 2021 |title=Vučić: Spremni smo da prihvatimo predloge EP, ma koliko to bilo teško za nas |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/vucic-spremni-smo-da-prihvatimo-predloge-ep-ma-koliko-to-bilo-tesko-za-nas/ |access-date=21 September 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> Opposition political parties rejected the draft and soon after abandoned the dialogues.<ref>{{Cite web |date=18 September 2021 |title=Veći deo opozicije odbacuje dokument o izbornim uslovima |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/veci-deo-opozicije-odbacuje-dokument-o-izbornim-uslovima/ |access-date=21 September 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref name=":12">{{Cite web |date=18 September 2021 |title=Međustranački dijalog: Vlast prihvatila formiranje tela za praćenje medija |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/medjustranacki-dijalog-vlast-prihvatila-formiranje-tela-za-pracenje-medija/ |access-date=21 September 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> Dačić announced that the government accepted the suggestions of forming a media monitoring body and the change in the composition of the Republic Electoral Commission (RIK).<ref name=":12" /> On 20 September 2021, Dačić stated that the inter-party dialogue with the participation of delegators from the European Parliament had ended.<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 September 2021 |title=Dačić: Međustranački dijalog je završen |url=https://nova.rs/vesti/politika/dacic-medjustranacki-dijalog-je-zavrsen/ |access-date=21 September 2021 |website=NOVA portal |language=sr-RS}}</ref>

On 27 August 2021, Dačić announced that the [[electoral threshold]] would remain at 3%.<ref>{{Cite web|date=27 August 2021|title=Dačić: Ostaje izborni cenzus od tri odsto, dijalog bez stranaca bolje napreduje|url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/dacic-o-medjustranackom-dijalogu-2/|access-date=2 September 2021|website=N1|language=sr-RS}}</ref> In early September 2021, political parties that took part in work board dialogues had received the draft document regarding the improvement of electoral conditions.<ref name=":7">{{Cite web |last=Gajić |first=Petar |date=2 September 2021 |title=Predstavljena radna verzija završnog dokumenta u dijalogu, čekaju se amandmani |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/predstavljena-radna-verzija-zavrsnog-dokumenta-u-dijalogu-obradovic-bez-pomaka/ |access-date=2 September 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> [[Democratic Party of Serbia]] (DSS), [[Dveri]], and [[POKS]] gave mixed responses to the document.<ref name=":8">{{Cite web |date=2 September 2021 |title=Mediji najavljuju spojene izbore 3. aprila, Šulkić (DSS) kaže da taj datum nije deo sporazuma |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/mediji-najavljuju-spojene-izbore-3-aprila-sulkic-dss-kaze-da-taj-datum-nije-deo-sporazuma/ |access-date=2 September 2021 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2 September 2021 |title=Obradović o ponudi vlasti: Promene u sastavu REM-a nisu predložene |url=https://nova.rs/vesti/politika/obradovic-o-ponudi-vlasti-promene-u-sastavu-rem-a-nisu-predlozene/ |access-date=2 September 2021 |website=NOVA portal |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2 September 2021 |title=Gojković: Prijatno sam iznenađen predlogom vlasti o izbornim uslovima |url=https://nova.rs/vesti/politika/gojkovic-prijatno-sam-iznenadjen-predlogom-vlasti-o-izbornim-uslovima/ |access-date=2 September 2021 |website=NOVA portal |language=sr-RS}}</ref> The signatory meeting was initially supposed to be held on 11 September, although it was later delayed to late October.<ref>{{cite web |last=Popović |first=Aleksandra |date=11 August 2021 |title=Sporazum vlasti i opozicije o izbornim uslovima moguć u septembru |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/sporazum-vlasti-i-opozicije-o-izbornim-uslovima-moguc-u-septembru/ |access-date=30 August 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr}}</ref><ref name=":11">{{Cite web |date=10 September 2021 |title=Odložen sastanak opozicije sa Vučićem, dogovor još daleko |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/odlozen-sastanak-opozicije-sa-vucicem-dogovor-jos-daleko/ |access-date=13 September 2021 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref> Representatives of DSS, Dveri, POKS, [[Enough is Enough (party)|Enough is Enough]] (DJB), [[Healthy Serbia]] (ZS), and [[Serbian Radical Party]] (SRS) took part in the signatory meeting which was held on 29 October 2021.<ref name=":13">{{Cite web |date=29 October 2021 |title=Danas u Skupštini Srbije potpisivanje Sporazuma o unapređenju izbornih uslova |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/danas-u-skupstini-srbije-potpisivanje-sporazuma-o-unapredjenju-izbornih-uslova/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029221730/https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/danas-u-skupstini-srbije-potpisivanje-sporazuma-o-unapredjenju-izbornih-uslova/ |archive-date=29 October 2021 |access-date=29 October 2021 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref> In the final document, it was agreed that the minimum number of collected signatories for minority ballot lists would be lowered from 10,000 to 5,000; more funding would be given to participants in elections; the obligation to post a financial guarantee for the participants would be abolished; the right of free use of all halls of local communities for pre-election activities for opposition parties would be given; electoral campaigns would be prohibited from ten days before the election date; fees would be increased for [[polling station]] staff; and the Regulatory Body of Electronic Media (REM) and Election Commission would receive composition changes.<ref name=":14">{{Cite web |last=Popović |first=Aleksandra |date=29 October 2021 |title=Sporazum sa vlašću: Dveri i DJB potpisali, ali nisu sasvim zadovoljni |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/sporazum-sa-vlascu-dveri-i-djb-potpisali-ali-nisu-sasvim-zadovoljni/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029220229/https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/sporazum-sa-vlascu-dveri-i-djb-potpisali-ali-nisu-sasvim-zadovoljni/ |archive-date=29 October 2021 |access-date=29 October 2021 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref name=":5" /> SNS declined to organise parliamentary and presidential elections on different days and to change rules regarding the ballot representatives, although it had offered to not open any "roads and factories" a week before the election date.<ref name=":7" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=2 September 2021 |title=Ponuda vlasti opoziciji: Sedam dana pre izbora bez otvaranja puteva i fabrika |url=https://nova.rs/vesti/politika/ponuda-vlasti-opoziciji-sedam-dana-pre-izbora-bez-otvaranja-puteva-i-fabrika/ |access-date=2 September 2021 |website=NOVA portal |language=sr-RS}}</ref>

=== Constitutional referendum ===
{{further|2022 Serbian constitutional referendum}}
Shortly after the 2020 parliamentary election, the government submitted a proposal to change the constitution to the [[National Assembly (Serbia)|National Assembly]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 July 2020 |title=Predlog za promenu Ustava Republike Srbije |url=http://www.parlament.gov.rs/upload/archive/files/cir/pdf/predlozi_zakona/2020/1984-20.pdf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201216180242/http://www.parlament.gov.rs/upload/archive/files/cir/pdf/predlozi_zakona/2020/1984-20.pdf |archive-date=16 December 2020 |website=National Assembly of the Republic of Serbia |location=Belgrade}}</ref> The National Assembly adopted the proposal in December 2020, while the document received further amendments up to June 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 December 2020 |title=Vlada predlaže promenu Ustava u delu koji se odnosi na sudove i javna tužilaštva |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/promenjen-ustav-u-delu-koji-se-odnosi-na-sudove-i-javna-tuzilastva/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211130214845/https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/promenjen-ustav-u-delu-koji-se-odnosi-na-sudove-i-javna-tuzilastva/ |archive-date=30 November 2021 |access-date=30 November 2021 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref name="jdcdanas">{{cite web |date=7 June 2021 |title=Poslanici usvojili Predlog za promenu Ustava Srbije |url=https://www.danas.rs/politika/poslanici-pred-glasanje-razlicito-o-predlogu-za-promenu-ustava/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr}}</ref> For the proposed changes to be adopted, a referendum needed to take place;<ref name="BBC1">{{cite web |last=Maksimović |first=Sandra |date=7 July 2021 |title=Srbija, pravosuđe i evropske integracije: Zašto se menja Ustav |url=https://www.bbc.com/serbian/lat/srbija-57147823 |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=BBC News |language=sr}}</ref> it was initially announced that it would be held in fall of 2021, although the date was later settled for 16 January 2022.<ref>{{cite web |date=6 July 2021 |title=Dačić: Referendum o promeni Ustava možda na jesen |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/dacic-referendum-o-promeni-ustava-mozda-na-jesen/ |access-date=30 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=9 November 2021 |title=Dačić: Raspisivanje referenduma o promeni Ustava najverovatnije u januaru |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/dacic-raspisivanje-referenduma-o-promeni-ustava-najverovatnije-u-januaru/ |access-date=25 November 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> In November 2021, changes to the law on referendum and people's initiative were adopted by the National Assembly;<ref name=":16">{{Cite news |date=16 November 2021 |title=Dačić: Skupština Srbije 25. novembra o zakonu o referendumu o ustavnim promenama |url=https://www.slobodnaevropa.org/a/dacic-ustavnepromene-skupstina/31564351.html |access-date=25 November 2021 |website=Radio Slobodna Evropa |language=sh}}</ref> the changes would abolish the 50% turnout that was needed for referendums to be considered valid.<ref>{{Cite web |date=9 November 2021 |title=Vlada usvojila Predlog zakona o referendumu, ukida se cenzus |url=http://www.rts.rs/page/stories/sr/story/9/politika/4583023/vlada-cenzus-referendum.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211109154350/https://www.rts.rs/page/stories/sr/story/9/politika/4583023/vlada-cenzus-referendum.html |archive-date=9 November 2021 |access-date=25 November 2021 |website=Radio Television of Serbia |language=sr}}</ref> Vučić signed the law on 25 November, although the law was also amended in December 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |date=29 November 2021 |title=Zakon o referendumu stupio na snagu, Vučić "misli da ga je potpisao" |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/zakon-o-referendumu-stupio-na-snagu-vucic-misli-da-ga-je-potpisao/ |access-date=29 November 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kovačević |first=Emina |date=10 December 2021 |title=Skupština jednoglasno usvojila izmene Zakona o referendumu |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/skupstina-srbije-o-izmenama-zakona-o-referendumu-i-narodnoj-inicijativi/ |access-date=13 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref>

SNS and SPS voiced their support for the constitutional changes,<ref>{{Cite web |date=12 January 2022 |title=Vučić: Glasaću 'da' na referendumu; da nam se ne ponovi onaj užas iz 2009. godine |url=https://www.b92.net/info/vesti/index.php?yyyy=2022&mm=01&dd=12&nav_category=11&nav_id=2087165 |access-date=12 January 2022 |website=B92 |language=sr}}</ref><ref name=":62">{{Cite web |last=Nikolin |first=Gorica |date=13 January 2022 |title=Novosadska politika: Ko glasa "da", ko glasa "ne", a ko će bojkotovati predstojeći referendum? |url=https://www.021.rs/story/Novi-Sad/Vesti/294805/Novosadska-politika-Ko-glasa-da-ko-glasa-ne-a-ko-ce-bojkotovati-predstojeci-referendum.html |access-date=13 January 2022 |website=021.rs |language=sr}}</ref> while the opposition were divided in opinion. A majority of opposition parties stated their opposition to the changes and called for citizens to vote against the proposed changes,<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 June 2021 |title=Dveri i PSG će pozvati na bojkot ustavnog referenduma |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/dveri-i-psg-ce-pozvati-na-bojkot-ustavnog-referenduma/ |access-date=25 January 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=23 December 2021 |title=Ne davimo Beograd pozvao građane da na referendumu glasaju protiv promena Ustava |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/ne-davimo-beograd-pozvao-gradjane-da-na-referendumu-glasaju-protiv-promena-ustava/ |access-date=26 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Valtner |first=Lidija |date=2 December 2021 |title=Desnica se mobiliše za izbore glasanjem protiv ustavnih izmena |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/desnica-se-mobilise-za-izbore-glasanjem-protiv-ustavnih-izmena/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211202080919/https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/desnica-se-mobilise-za-izbore-glasanjem-protiv-ustavnih-izmena/ |archive-date=2 December 2021 |access-date=6 December 2021 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref> while SSP, [[Democratic Party (Serbia)|Democratic Party]] (DS), and [[Party of Democratic Action of Sandžak]] (SDAS) called to boycott the referendum.<ref>{{Cite web |date=12 November 2021 |title=Đilas: Referendum o ustavnim promenama za SSP ne postoji, nelegitiman je kao i Skupština |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/djilas-referendum-o-ustavnim-promenama-za-ssp-ne-postoji-nelegitiman-je-kao-i-skupstina/ |access-date=25 January 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=5 January 2022 |title=Ugljanin: Nećemo učestvovati na referendumu i stvaranju iluzije da je stanje u Srbiji normalno |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/ugljanin-necemo-ucestvovati-na-referendumu-i-stvaranju-iluzije-da-je-stanje-u-srbiji-normalno/ |archive-url=|archive-date= |access-date=12 January 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref> Nevertheless, analysts had concluded that the campaign would be restrained due to the April 2022 general election and that a possibility of abuse of the referendum would be possible.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nešić |first=Nenad |date=8 December 2021 |title=Opozicija i referendum o promeni Ustava: Ignorisati ili ne, pitanje je sad |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/opozicija-i-referendum-o-promeni-ustava-ignorisati-ili-ne-pitanje-je-sad/ |access-date=14 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Đurić |first=Vanja |date=11 December 2021 |title=Stručnjaci: Izmene zakona dobre i ključne, ali ima još prostora za zloupotrebu |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/strucnjaci-izmene-zakona-dobre-i-kljucne-ali-ima-jos-prostora-za-zloupotrebu/ |access-date=12 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> In the end, a majority of voters voted in favour of changes, although the turnout was reported to be only 30%.<ref name=":112">{{Cite web |date=17 January 2022 |title=RIK: "Da" zaokružilo 59,71 odsto glasalih, "ne" 39,26 odsto |url=http://www.tanjug.rs/full-view.aspx?izb=711353 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220117115107/http://tanjug.rs/full-view.aspx?izb=711353 |archive-date=17 January 2022 |access-date=17 January 2022 |website=Tanjug |language=sr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=16 January 2022 |title=Gošće N1: Očekivana izlaznost, vlast olako pristupila referendumu |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/gosce-n1-ocekivana-izlaznost-vlast-olako-pristupila-referendumu/ |access-date=17 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> A majority of voters in [[Belgrade]], [[Novi Sad]] and [[Niš]] voted against the proposed changes.<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 January 2022 |title=U Beogradu većina izašlih ipak glasala za "ne" |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/u-beogradu-vecina-izaslih-ipak-glasala-za-ne/ |access-date=17 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=17 January 2022 |title=I Niš rekao "ne" ustavnim promenama – 53,28 odsto izašlih glasalo protiv |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/i-nis-rekao-ne-ustavnim-promenama-5328-glasalo-protiv/ |access-date=17 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=16 January 2022 |title=Prvi preliminarni rezultati: Novi Sad rekao "Ne" na referendumu |url=https://www.021.rs/story/Novi-Sad/Vesti/295256/Prvi-preliminarni-rezultati-Novi-Sad-rekao-Ne-na-referendumu.html |access-date=17 January 2022 |website=021.rs |language=sr}}</ref> Irregularities were reported at voting stations, while some non-governmental organisations, such as the Centre for Research, Transparency, and Accountability (CRTA), and opposition parties, such as DJB, claimed voter fraud.<ref>{{Cite web|date=16 January 2022|title=CRTA: Ubacivali listiće u Novom Pazaru|url=https://nova.rs/vesti/politika/crta-ubacivali-listice-u-novom-pazaru/|access-date=17 January 2022|website=NOVA portal|language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Đurić|first=Vanja|date=20 January 2022|title=Radulović: Vlast falsifikovala 600.000 glasova "da" na referendumu|url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/radulovic-vlast-falsifikovala-600-000-glasova-da-na-referendumu/|access-date=23 January 2022|website=N1|language=sr-RS}}</ref>

=== Environmental protests ===
[[File:Environmental protests in Belgrade, 11 December 2021.jpg|thumb|alt=Demonstrators roadblocking on the Gazela Bridge, Belgrade on 11 December 2021|Protests in Belgrade on 11 December 2021]]
{{further|2021–2022 Serbian environmental protests}}
[[Rio Tinto (corporation)|Rio Tinto]], an Anglo-Australian mining corporation, had operated in Serbia since 2004 and it explored mines near the [[Jadar (Drina)|Jadar Valley]].<ref name=":110">{{Cite web |last=Anđelković |first=Nataša |date=20 January 2021 |title=Jadarit, Rio Tinto i šta o tome treba da znate |url=https://www.bbc.com/serbian/lat/srbija-55646178 |access-date=4 December 2021 |website=BBC News |language=sr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=3 December 2021 |title=Tadić: Rio Tinto došao u vreme Koštuničine vlasti, Vučić razvodnjava pritisak |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/boris-tadic-o-dolasku-rio-tinta-u-srbiju/ |access-date=4 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr}}</ref> The government of Serbia signed an agreement in 2017 to implement Project Jadar, which would give Rio Tinto permission to exploit the [[jadarite]] [[Silicate mineral|mineral]].<ref name=":110" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jevtović |first=Mirjana |date=24 June 2017 |title=Projekat Jadar – Kompanija Rio Tinto nastavlja ulaganje u rudnik litijuma kod Loznice |url=https://insajder.net/sr/sajt/vazno/6041/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211204172537/https://insajder.net/sr/sajt/vazno/6041/ |archive-date=4 December 2021 |access-date=4 December 2021 |website=Insajder |language=sr}}</ref> Environmental protests in Serbia were held as early as in January 2021,<ref>{{Cite web |date=6 January 2021 |title=Protest za bezopasan vazduh zakazan za 10. januar u Beogradu |url=https://balkangreenenergynews.com/rs/protest-za-bezopasan-vazduh-zakazan-za-10-januar-u-beogradu/ |access-date=4 December 2021 |website=Balkan Green Energy News |language=sr-RS}}</ref> although the protests that began in September 2021 garnered national attention.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 August 2021 |title=Protest Ustanak za opstanak - narod protiv Rio Tinta zakazan za 11. septembar u Beogradu |url=https://balkangreenenergynews.com/rs/protest-ustanak-za-opstanak-narod-protiv-rio-tinta-zakazan-za-11-septembar-u-beogradu/ |access-date=4 December 2021 |website=Balkan Green Energy News |language=sr-RS}}</ref> [[Environmental organisations]] and groups demanded the government to withdraw the referendum and expropriation laws, as well as to cancel Project Jadar.<ref>{{Cite web |date=12 April 2021 |title=Premijerka: Spremili smo odgovore na sve zahteve sa ekološkog protesta |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/brnabic-spremili-smo-odgovore-na-sve-zahteve-sa-ekoloskog-protesta/ |access-date=4 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=27 November 2021 |title=Serbian anti-mining activists block roads to protest against new laws |language=en |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/china/serbian-anti-mining-activists-block-roads-protest-against-new-laws-2021-11-27/ |access-date=6 December 2021}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Savić |first=Danilo |date=11 September 2021 |title=Danas je Ekološki ustanak: Gde i kada, ko će i o čemu govoriti |url=https://nova.rs/vesti/drustvo/danas-je-ekoloski-ustanak-gde-i-kada-ko-ce-i-o-cemu-govoriti/ |access-date=4 December 2021 |website=NOVA portal |language=sr-RS}}</ref> The protests resumed in November 2021, and peaked in late November and early December 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |date=27 November 2021 |title=Ekološki ustanak: Blokade paralisale Beograd, uspele preko očekivanja |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/ekoloski-ustanak-blokade-paralisale-beograd-uspele-preko-ocekivanja/ |access-date=6 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=1 December 2021 |title=U subotu blokade puteva duže od sat vremena i protest advokata |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/u-subotu-blokade-puteva-duze-od-sat-vremena-i-protest-advokata/ |access-date=10 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> A series of [[Traffic obstruction|roadblocks]] protests were held during that period and they ended up turning violent.<ref>{{Cite web |date=26 November 2021 |title=Ekološki ustanak najavio blokadu puteva u Srbiji u subotu |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/ekoloski-ustanak-poziva-na-blokadu-puteva-u-srbiji-u-subotu/ |access-date=5 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref name=":92">{{Cite web |date=28 November 2021 |title=Environmental protests and roadblocks across Serbia, masked men attack citizens |url=https://europeanwesternbalkans.com/2021/11/28/environmental-protests-and-roadblocks-across-serbia-masked-men-attack-citizens/ |access-date=6 December 2021 |website=European Western Balkans |language=en-US}}</ref> Following the protests in early December 2021, the government withdrew the expropriation law and abolished the spatial plan for Project Jadar.<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 December 2021 |title=Vučić ispunio sve zahteve protesta: Izašao sam u susret narodu, ne političarima |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/vucic-zakon-o-eksproprijaciji-protesti-rio-tinto-zagadjenje-srbija/ |access-date=12 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=18 January 2022 |title=Brnabić: U ovom trenutku za mene je projekat "Jadar" gotov |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/brnabic-u-ovom-trenutku-za-mene-je-projekat-jadar-gotov/ |access-date=21 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> Protests were organised up to 15 February 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 January 2022 |title=Završene blokade u Beogradu i Novom Sadu, najavljena radikalizacija protesta |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/blog-uzivo-nove-blokade-protiv-rio-tinta-i-rudnika-litijuma-u-srbiji/ |access-date=10 January 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=15 February 2022 |title=Police cordon stops environmental activists' march |url=https://rs.n1info.com/english/news/police-cordon-stops-environmental-activists-march/ |access-date=16 February 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> Protests in support were also organised internationally.<ref>{{Cite web |date=4 December 2021 |title=Protesti podrške u Njujorku, Berlinu, Parizu: "Ne" Rio Tintu, SNS marš sa vlasti |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/protesti-podrske-u-njujorku-berlinu-parizu/ |access-date=10 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> The government criticised the protests and its organisers.<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 September 2021 |title=Mihajlović: Ekološki skup iskorišćen za promociju pojedinih koji žele u politiku |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/mihajlovic-ekoloski-skup-iskoriscen-za-promociju-pojedinih-koji-zele-u-politiku/ |access-date=4 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=11 December 2021 |title=Mihajlović: Nastavak zloupotrebe teme životne sredine u predizbornoj kampanji |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/mihajlovic-nastavak-zloupotrebe-teme-zivotne-sredine-u-predizbornoj-kampanji/ |access-date=12 December 2021 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref>

== Electoral system ==
The [[president of Serbia]] is elected using the [[two-round system]] and has a term of five years, although it is limited to two terms in any order of service.<ref>{{Cite web|title=O izborima za predsednika Republike|url=https://www.rik.parlament.gov.rs/tekst/sr/78/o-izborima-za-predsednika-republike.php|url-status=live|access-date=29 October 2021|website=www.rik.parlament.gov.rs|language=sr|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200607154107/https://www.rik.parlament.gov.rs/tekst/sr/78/o-izborima-za-predsednika-republike.php |archive-date=7 June 2020 }}</ref><ref name="electionguide">{{cite web|date=2 April 2017|title=ElectionGuide, Serbia|url=https://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2709/|access-date=30 January 2022|website=ElectionGuide}}</ref> If no candidate receives a majority of the vote in the first round, a second is held.<ref name="electionguide" /> An official candidate needs to collect 10,000 signatures, be at least 18 years old and possess Serbian citizenship. It is not necessary to have been born in Serbia.<ref name=":0" />

The 250 [[Member of parliament|members]] of the [[National Assembly (Serbia)|National Assembly]] are elected by [[closed-list]] [[proportional representation]] from a single nationwide constituency. Seats are allocated using the [[d'Hondt method]] with an [[electoral threshold]] of 3% of all votes cast,<ref>{{cite web|last=Bjelotomić|first=Snežana|date=13 January 2020|title=Electoral threshold reduced to 3%|url=https://www.serbianmonitor.com/en/electoral-threshold-reduced-to-3/|website=Serbian Monitor}}</ref> although the threshold is waived for [[Minority group|ethnic minority]] parties.<ref name=":0">{{cite web|date=2016|title=Electoral system of Serbia|url=http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2355_B.htm|access-date=30 January 2022|website=Inter-Parliamentary Union}}</ref> Minority ballots need at least 5,000 signatories to qualify on the ballot while non-minority ballots need 10,000.<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Milovančević |first=Vojislav |date=29 October 2021 |title=Detalji sporazuma Vučića i opozicije iz Skupštine |url=https://nova.rs/vesti/politika/ekskluzivno-detalji-sporazuma-vucica-i-opozicije-iz-skupstine/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029113154/https://nova.rs/vesti/politika/ekskluzivno-detalji-sporazuma-vucica-i-opozicije-iz-skupstine/ |archive-date=29 October 2021 |access-date=29 October 2021 |website=NOVA portal |language=sr-RS}}</ref> As of 2020, 40% of the candidates on the electoral lists must be female.<ref>{{cite web|date=8 February 2022|title=Cenzus tri odsto, na listama 40 procenata žena|url=https://www.rts.rs/page/stories/sr/story/9/politika/3844903/cenzus-tri-odsto-na-listama-40-procenata-zena.html|access-date=29 January 2022|website=Radio Television of Serbia|language=sr}}</ref>

On 15 February 2022, Dačić called local elections to be held, while Vučić then dissolved the National Assembly and called parliamentary elections to be held on 3 April 2022.<ref name=":19">{{Cite web |date=15 February 2022 |title=Vučić raspisao vanredne parlamentarne izbore za 3. april: Neka su vam srećni! |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/vucic-raspisao-vanredne-parlamentarne-izbore-za-3-april/ |access-date=15 February 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS |archive-date=15 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220215181233/https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/vucic-raspisao-vanredne-parlamentarne-izbore-za-3-april/ |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=14 February 2022 |title=U utorak se raspušta Skupština, Dačić raspisuje lokalne izbore |url=https://rs.n1info.com/vesti/dacic-u-utorak-se-raspusta-narodna-skupstina/ |access-date=15 February 2022 |website=N1 |language=sr-RS}}</ref> Presidential elections were called on 2 March.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2 March 2022 |title=Ivica Dačić raspisao predsedničke izbore |url=https://www.rts.rs/page/stories/sr/Izbori2022/story/3364/vesti/4722835/ivica-dacic-izbori-predsednicki-raspisivanje.html |access-date=2 March 2022 |website=Radio Television of Serbia |language=sr-RS}}</ref> The [[Serbian diaspora]] was able to vote in 34 countries, although, elections in [[Canada]], the [[United Kingdom]], and the [[United States]] were held on 2 April due to time zone differences.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 March 2022 |title=RIK usvojio odluke za glasanje u inostranstvu |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/izbori22/rik-usvojio-odluke-za-glasanje-u-inostranstvu/ |access-date=14 March 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=13 March 2022 |title=26. sednica Republičke izborne komisije |url=https://www.rik.parlament.gov.rs/vest/sr/108441/26-sednica-republicke-izborne-komisije.php |access-date=14 March 2022 |website=Republic Electoral Commission |language=sr}}</ref> [[Albin Kurti]], the [[Prime Minister of Kosovo|prime minister of Kosovo]], stated that his government would try to find a solution for the elections with ambassadors of European Union, and he also expressed his support that Kosovar Serbs who possess [[Serbian citizenship]] should have the right to vote in the general election.<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 March 2022 |title=Kurti: Naći ćemo rešenje za srpske izbore |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/izbori22/kurti-naci-cemo-resenje-za-srpske-izbore/ |access-date=15 March 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Valtner |first=Lidija |date=15 March 2022 |title=Hoće li biti srpskih izbora na Kosovu: Javnost i opozicija protiv, Kurti se možda povuče u poslednjem trenutku |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/hoce-li-biti-srpskih-izbora-na-kosovu-javnost-i-opozicija-protiv-kurti-se-mozda-povuce-u-poslednjem-trenutku/ |access-date=15 March 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref> RIK had announced that Kosovar Serbs would have the right to vote in four municipalities in Serbia.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 March 2022 |title=RIK odredio četiri opštine u Srbiji gde će građani sa Kosova moći da glasaju |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/rik-odredio-cetiri-opstine-u-srbiji-gde-ce-gradjani-sa-kosova-moci-da-glasaju/ |access-date=27 March 2022 |website=Danas |language=sr-RS}}</ref>

